  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">USAID&#x2019;s We&#x2019;re Reading Activity (Sasa Tunasoma ya USAID)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Democratic Republic of the Congo):  Congolese children and youth, particularly the most marginalized, face multiple barriers to acquiring foundational literacy skills. Despite these challenges, Congolese value education and, when resources and education opportunities are available, families prioritize education. Education is valued as a pathway out of cycles of poverty, conflict, and instability. The Congolese government has identified improving education as a top priority...With FY 2022 funds, USAID&apos;s We&apos;re Reading will leverage USAID&apos;s prior experience supporting foundational learning to ensure that children aged six to nine in select marginalized and underserved zones of South Kivu gain foundational literacy and social and emotional skills, enabling them to function in school at a second grade level or higher...USAID will achieve results by engaging community leaders and parents. It will provide safe spaces, contextually-relevant materials, and multiple learning pathways for children who have had limited access to formal schooling, especially girls, indigenous children (pygmies), and children with disabilities. This intervention adopts a modular implementation approach, whereby result packages may be activated or deactivated based on contextual and operational constraints, opportunities, and learning results...We&apos;re Reading will advance the Integrated Country Strategy via Mission Objective 3.2 (Improve public institutions&apos; responsiveness, respect for human rights and basic freedoms, and accountability, including through strengthening education overall) by improving education services delivery, increasing access to education services, and strengthening partnership between education actors. It addresses the Administration policy priority of diversity, equity, and inclusion by targeting marginalized groups, including girls, indigenous children, and children with disabilities..</narrative>
  </description>
